In this lesson, we'll find the midpoint of a line interval, which is just the point sitting exactly halfway between two other points on the plane. The big idea is beautifully simple. Whenever you have two points, one with an x and a y, and the other with an x and a y, you just average the x's and you average the y's. So you add the two x coordinates together and divide by two, and you do the same with the two y coordinates. That gives you the halfway point. And here's a nice way to check your work. The midpoint should be exactly the same distance from each of the two endpoints, so if you measure both sides, they'll match up. This trick comes in handy more often than you'd think. You can use it to find the centre of a circle that passes through two points, or to locate the middle marker between two spots on a number line. It's also useful in geometry proofs whenever you need a centre of symmetry. Now let's see it in action.